From 56c5bcd7481510fe78180f54b65eb27b511504e5 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Neil Johnson <neil.johnson71@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 19 Aug 2026 13:56:30 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] asxxsrc: make the source-line input buffer (ib[]/ic[])
 dynamically sized

83a99c1 fixed aslex.c's nxtline() to actually use ib[]'s/ic[]'s full
declared capacity (NINPUT*2, 759 usable characters) instead of just
NINPUT (379) - but that 759-character capacity was itself never a
considered answer to "how long can a source line legitimately be",
just headroom that happened to already exist for in-place .define
substitution growth (see replace() in aslex.c and macrosub() in
asmcro.c). A source line at or past 760 characters still hits the
same class of failure 83a99c1 fixed: fgets()/fgetm() truncate mid-line
without reaching the newline, and the unread remainder of the line
is then handed to the lexer as if it were a new statement, typically
producing a spurious directive/mnemonic error.

There's no principled fixed constant to pick here - a chained
expression, a long .db/.byte data initializer, or any other
mechanically-generated line can legitimately be arbitrarily long.
So instead of raising the constant again, ib[]/ic[] are now grown
on demand:

  - ib[] and ic[] change from fixed NINPUT*2-sized arrays to
    malloc()'d buffers, sized by a new global, ibsize (initially
    NINPUT*2, matching the previous capacity exactly - no behavior
    change for any line under the old limit).
  - A new function, fgetline() in aslex.c, replaces the direct
    fgets() calls in nxtline()'s T_ASM/T_INCL cases. It reads into
    ib[] as before, but if a line fills the buffer without reaching
    its terminating newline, ib[]/ic[] are both realloc()'d to twice
    their current size and reading continues where it left off,
    repeating until the newline (or end of file) is found.
  - nxtline()'s T_MACRO case (fgetm()) now passes the current ibsize
    instead of a fixed length; macro body text is stored via
    mstring(), which already allocates exactly strlen()+1 bytes
    per line with no fixed cap, so this is just making the copy
    destination size track ib[]'s actual (monotonically
    nondecreasing) capacity rather than a stale constant.

One additional bug surfaced by actually exercising this rather than
just reasoning about it: main() caches a pointer to whichever of
ib[]/ic[] is selected for listing (il = bflag ? ib : ic) exactly
once, before the assembler's pass loop starts. Since realloc() is
free to move a block, the first time fgetline() actually grows the
buffers, il was left pointing at freed memory for the rest of the
run - listing output for the growing line (and only that line) came
out as garbage. fgetline() now refreshes il itself whenever it grows
ib[]/ic[], immediately after the realloc()s that could have moved
them.

Deliberately out of scope: replace()'s and macrosub()'s own
substitution-growth bound checks (both still compare against the
literal NINPUT*2 - 1, unchanged). Both copy through a local,
fixed-size NINPUT*2 scratch buffer (str[]/xrgstr[]) while ip points
mid-line into ib[]; widening their limit to track ibsize without
also resizing those scratch buffers (or reworking their ip-relative
pointer arithmetic to survive a realloc) would trade one fixed limit
for a stack-buffer overflow, for a scenario - a single line combining
a huge machine-generated length with an in-place .define expansion -
this report never demonstrated. A genuinely long raw source line, the
actual reported problem, no longer has any assembler-imposed ceiling.

Verified with a standalone asz80 built from asxxsrc/*.c + asz80/*.c:
- Confirmed the prior (post-83a99c1) 759-character ceiling: a
  759-character comment line assembles cleanly, 760 characters
  produces the same spurious directive/mnemonic error 83a99c1 fixed
  for the previous, smaller threshold.
- With this fix: comment lines of 760, 1135, 2000, and 5000
  characters (spanning zero, one, and multiple buffer-doubling
  events) all assemble cleanly; the full text of each survives intact
  in the listing (checked by counting the exact character run in each
  case), with no ceiling in sight short of available memory.
- Also verified with genuine non-comment content: a single .db
  directive line emitting 400 comma-separated byte values (1384
  characters) assembles correctly - right byte count, right values,
  and the following instruction lands at the correct address
  immediately afterward - confirming the fix isn't comment-specific.
- Rebuilt with -fsanitize=address,undefined and reran all of the
  above plus the existing asz80/t80.bat and asz80/tz80.bat self-tests:
  no sanitizer reports.
- Ran t80.bat and tz80.bat (asz80 -gloaxf, aslink -xu, asxscn on the
  resulting .lst/.rst) against a plain build of this fix and against
  an unmodified baseline built from the same source tree: byte-for-
  byte identical .lst/.rst output in both cases (apart from
  timestamps), including the two pre-existing, unrelated asxscn "code
  difference" reports in t80 (an HD64180 TST addressing quirk). No
  regressions.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 asxxsrc/asdata.c |  8 +++--
 asxxsrc/aslex.c  | 78 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++--
 asxxsrc/asmain.c | 12 ++++++++
 asxxsrc/asxxxx.h |  9 ++++--
 4 files changed, 100 insertions(+), 7 deletions(-)

